aces so once  you soak through uh  you're okay if you're moving but once  you stop and there's a lot of stopping  uh  the chill factor is quite high with the  with the the draft through the cave and  so it is easy to get cold so you need to  keep your spirits up and you know we  look after each other you know that's  kind of there's a lot of camaraderie  with doing this and uh  you know it's just  keep your spirits up remain mentally  strong and just stay focused on what  we're doing it is a minute by minute  thing um and we don't break it down  beyond what we're doing really other  than you know the team that are sort of  working ahead um  and then you know  eventually one of those moments is the  moment when we exit the cave  did you know it was always going to be a  good outcome  i think for the most part we we have to  we know what we're doing and we know  the condition of the patient  we certainly we have our team doctors  and medics  so we're aware of of of his or her  condition um  and we go in with the intention of  coming out with a successful outcome you  know that  it's it's it's you know we stay positive  stay focused  and get the job done  i think you had a bit of interaction  with the with the cave of the patient  didn't you what was his condition like  and did he manage to say anything  uh yeah it's right it's a long time for  anybody to be um strapped into a  stretcher  and immobilized especially with injuries  so um  you know you take that into account but  but  there were times when he was um  quite perky he's a very strong character  um  uh yeah he was okay he was certainly  very happy to be out of the cave we  wouldn't be  what did you say to him  actually as we came out of the cave um i  was just fortunate to be in exactly the  right position to be able to lean into  him and  say you're out of the cave now it was it  was a it was a great moment  and how did he react did he smile or was  he just so relieved  you can see the the you can see he was